WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Least privilege depends on runtime context as much as assigned permissions.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:11.000
A script may appear narrow when read in isolation, but the environment around it can change what it does.

3
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:15.660
PATH controls which executable runs when a command name is used.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:26.320
Environment variables can influence credentials, configuration locations, proxies, debug modes, language runtimes, temporary directories, and tool-specific behavior.

5
00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:30.980
The current working directory can change which files are read or written.

6
00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:36.700
Command lookup deserves attention because Bash scripts often call external tools by name.

7
00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:47.120
If PATH differs between an interactive shell, a cron job, a service, and a CI/CD runner, the script may execute a different binary than the author expected.

8
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:52.100
Defensive scripts should avoid relying on accidental lookup behavior for sensitive workflows.

9
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:57.640
They should make important assumptions explicit and keep the command path model understandable to reviewers.

10
00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:01.040
Environment variables can act like hidden inputs.

11
00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:03.700
Some are harmless convenience settings.

12
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:09.960
Others can change authentication, output, network routing, logging, or configuration.

13
00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:15.900
A script that inherits a large environment from a privileged context may carry more authority than intended.

14
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.400
Before launching unrelated helper tools, consider whether sensitive or authority-changing variables should be minimized.

15
00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:30.020
At the same time, avoid logging full environments because they can include secrets and operationally sensitive context.

16
00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:33.260
Working directory and helper trust matter too.

17
00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:38.160
A relative path can point somewhere different depending on how the script was started.

18
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:43.220
A helper script may have its own permissions, assumptions, and side effects.

19
00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:54.360
Review the runtime context as part of least privilege: which commands are called, where they come from, which variables influence them, where files are read and written, and what context is logged.

20
00:01:54.460 --> 00:01:59.100
The goal is predictable behavior with no accidental inherited power.

21
00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:06.080
Reviewers can verify important runtime assumptions and understand exactly how each command path is selected.
